[CENTER][SIZE="5"][B][GUIDE] How To Unlock the Bootloader LG G2 Mini LTE D625 (Tegra 4i)[/B][/SIZE]

[B][SIZE="4"][COLOR="Red"]WARNING: THIS MODIFIES THE BOOTLOADER — READ EVERYTHING BEFORE STARTING[/COLOR][/SIZE][/B][/CENTER]

[CODE]#include <std_disclaimer.h>
/*
 * Your warranty is now void.
 *
 * I am not responsible for bricked devices, dead SD cards, lost IMEI,
 * or any other damage. Back up everything and verify every hash before
 * flashing. YOU are choosing to make these modifications.
 */[/CODE]

[SIZE="4"][B]About[/B][/SIZE]

The LG G2 Mini LTE [B]D625[/B] uses an [B]NVIDIA Tegra 4i[/B], not a Snapdragon, and has no official unlock. LG's bootloader (EBT) refuses any recovery or boot image that is not signed by LG.

This guide installs a [B]patched EBT[/B]: [B]two instructions, three bytes[/B] changed so the signature check is skipped. The patch is applied with NVIDIA's own [B]nvflash[/B] from APX mode, which also updates the bootloader hash in the BCT. Android keeps booting normally, and fastboot then reports [B]unlocked: yes[/B] and [B]secure: no[/B].

[B]What you get:[/B]
[LIST]
[*]Unsigned recovery images boot, for example TWRP 3.1.1-1 for the D625.
[*][B]No data wipe:[/B] this is not [I]fastboot oem unlock[/I], and userdata is untouched.
[*]Fully reversible: the original EBT can be reinstalled the same way.
[/LIST]

[SIZE="4"][B]Supported firmware[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*][B]D625 - D62510c[/B] (Android 4.4.2, KOT49I): tested.
[/LIST]

[COLOR="Red"][B]Do not use this kit on any other firmware.[/B][/COLOR] Other D625 firmware versions have not been checked and may use a different bootloader. Step 4 compares your bootloader's SHA-256 with the D62510c one before anything is written; if it differs, stop. Tested on one D625.

[SIZE="4"][B]Download (everything in one zip)[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*][URL="https://androidfilehost.com/?fid=17900174959292981777"]LG-D625-Bootloader-Unlock-v2.zip[/URL] - 1.6 MB | MD5: d8c5cc27f011719c51b4ce7c1fe76640
[*]Mirror: [URL="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZRlPnMpLe6S9HZv-svuBCHOSEqAmcykg/view?usp=sharing"]Google Drive[/URL] (same file, same MD5)
[/LIST]

[B]Ready to flash:[/B] the kit includes the [B]patched D62510c bootloader[/B] ([I]ebt\ebt-patched-v2-D62510c.bin[/I]) and the [B]stock D62510c bootloader[/B] to go back ([I]ebt\ebt-stock-D62510c.bin[/I]). It also has nvflash 4.10.1800, the original D625 BCT, the Wiko Wax RAM server (already named [I]wiko-bootloader-server.bin[/I]), SHA256 checksums, and a README.txt with these same steps. No Python and no patching are needed. Unzip it and run every command below from its folder.

[B]Why not a TWRP zip or dd, like on the D620?[/B] On Tegra the bootloader is protected by a hash stored in the BCT, and only nvflash's managed download updates it. Writing EBT with dd or a recovery zip leaves the old hash in place, and the phone does not boot. That is why APX mode and nvflash are required.

[SIZE="4"][B]Requirements[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*]LG D625 on firmware [B]D62510c[/B], with the [B]battery inserted[/B] the whole time.
[*]A Windows PC. Python 3 is only needed for the optional "patch it yourself" route.
[*][B]adb and fastboot[/B] ([URL="https://developer.android.com/tools/releases/platform-tools"]Android SDK Platform-Tools[/URL]), and [B]USB debugging[/B] enabled on the phone (Settings > Developer options), for the checks in Step 6.
[*]The patch repository: [URL="https://github.com/chijure-android-system-development/lg-d625-tegra-bootloader-patch"]lg-d625-tegra-bootloader-patch[/URL]. It includes:
[LIST]
[*][B]tools/nvflash.exe[/B]: the original NVIDIA nvflash 4.10.1800 used for the test.
[*][B]bct/bct-original-slot.cfg[/B]: the original D625 BCT, needed to start nvflash.
[*][B]validate_and_patch_ebt.py[/B]: the offline patch generator.
[/LIST]
[*]The [B]Wiko Wax nvflash RAM server[/B]: [I]bootloader.bin[/I] from the Wiko Wax FlashCWM/nvflash package (see the [URL="https://forum.frandroid.com/topic/203892-recovery-twrp-2800-02/"]Wiko Wax TWRP thread[/URL]). DumpWAX and the RAM server are also covered in the [URL="https://forum.frandroid.com/topic/241543-tuto-rooter-changer-de-rom-sur-wiko-wax-4g/"]Wiko Wax root and ROM thread[/URL]. It is included in the kit above, but not in the GitHub repository. The copy used for testing is 718688 bytes, SHA-256:
[CODE]e6833eca87d3355e3017df9ee5ec6b3c5a275a3652e07eca949e8ac2df9116a5[/CODE]
The guide calls it [I]wiko-bootloader-server.bin[/I]. It only runs from RAM; it is never written to the phone.
[*]An APX USB driver on Windows (NVIDIA APX driver or WinUSB) that lets nvflash see the phone.
[/LIST]

Verify the files from the kit (or repository) folder in PowerShell:
[CODE](Get-FileHash tools/nvflash.exe -Algorithm SHA256).Hash
# 000FA264D6614A1D3490B81C0E8FBA5A72B0F70E74CA8141E108229DC7FAB466
(Get-FileHash bct/bct-original-slot.cfg -Algorithm SHA256).Hash
# 8583855D33606748415F65AE291A194500BAB1110C8D8CC34119C59854423193[/CODE]

[SIZE="4"][B]Partition numbers (nvflash)[/B][/SIZE]

On the D625, nvflash numbers are [B]EBT = 5[/B], [B]RBL = 4[/B] and [B]SOS (recovery) = 16[/B]. [COLOR="Red"][B]Do not use the Wiko Wax BCT or its partition numbers.[/B][/COLOR] The Wiko server and the LG bootloader are different files.

[SIZE="4"][B]Step 1 — Enter APX mode[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST=1]
[*]Power off the phone. Keep the battery in.
[*]Hold [B]Volume Up + Volume Down[/B] and plug in the USB cable.
[*]The screen stays black. Windows should detect an NVIDIA APX device; install the driver if needed.
[*]Open a console [B]as administrator[/B] in the repository folder.
[/LIST]

[SIZE="4"][B]Step 2 — Load the RAM server and read the partition table[/B][/SIZE]

[CODE].\tools\nvflash.exe --bct bct/bct-original-slot.cfg --getpartitiontable ptable.txt --bl wiko-bootloader-server.bin --go[/CODE]

Open [I]ptable.txt[/I] and confirm that partition 5 is EBT, 4 is RBL and 16 is SOS. [B]If it does not match, stop.[/B]

[SIZE="4"][B]Step 3 — Back up EBT, RBL and BCT (do not skip)[/B][/SIZE]

[CODE].\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --read 5 ebt-before.bin
.\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --read 4 rbl-before.bin
.\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --rawdeviceread 0 1024 bct-before.bin[/CODE]

These are read-only operations. [I]rawdeviceread[/I] uses 4096-byte nvflash sectors. Copy all three files somewhere safe; they are your only way back.

[SIZE="4"][B]Step 4 — Check that your bootloader is the D62510c one (do not skip)[/B][/SIZE]

The bootloader is the first 860160 bytes of the EBT partition. Hash it in PowerShell:
[CODE]$b = [IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("$PWD\ebt-before.bin")
[BitConverter]::ToString([Security.Cryptography.SHA256]::Create().ComputeHash($b, 0, 860160)).Replace('-','')[/CODE]

[LIST]
[*][B]10BC066B7F99256F87B05E82B5087C5C6F815A3E865DC094169176FCC69706F7[/B]: stock D62510c bootloader. Continue with Step 5.
[*][B]C2C787A409EF6FD3EA01F9225DCA4F40D81CF5164DE631F9BB067BF4FF43541B[/B]: already patched. You are done; go to Step 6.
[*][COLOR="Red"][B]Anything else: STOP.[/B][/COLOR] Your bootloader is different and this patch is not for it. Do not flash anything.
[/LIST]

[B]Optional, patch it yourself:[/B] instead of using the included file, extract your bootloader and run the offline script. It only accepts the stock D62510c bootloader and must produce the same file as the kit (SHA-256 c2c787a4…):
[CODE][IO.File]::WriteAllBytes("$PWD\bootloader-original.bin", $b[0..860159])
python validate_and_patch_ebt.py bootloader-original.bin ebt-patched-v2.bin[/CODE]

[SIZE="4"][B]Step 5 — Install the patched EBT[/B][/SIZE]

[CODE].\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --download 5 ebt\ebt-patched-v2-D62510c.bin --sync[/CODE]

[COLOR="Red"][B]Use exactly this managed --download … --sync command.[/B][/COLOR] Do [B]not[/B] write EBT with dd, --rawdevicewrite or any raw sector write, and do [B]not[/B] edit the BCT by hand. The managed download updates the bootloader hash and the BCT signature. A raw write skips that, and the BootROM then falls back to RBL or refuses to boot.

On the test unit, --sync ended with exit code 0 and the USB device disappeared. Unplug the phone, pull and reinsert the battery if needed, and power it on. Android should boot normally.

[COLOR="Red"][B]If Android does not boot,[/B][/COLOR] do not try anything else: enter APX again and follow [B]Restore the stock bootloader[/B] below.

[SIZE="4"][B]Step 6 — Verify[/B][/SIZE]

With Android running and ADB authorized:
[CODE]adb reboot bootloader
fastboot getvar unlocked
fastboot getvar secure[/CODE]

Expected: [B]unlocked: yes[/B] and [B]secure: no[/B]. You can now test a recovery without installing it:
[CODE]fastboot boot recovery.img[/CODE]

[B]Optional readback:[/B] enter APX again, repeat Step 2, then:
[CODE].\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --read 5 ebt-after.bin
.\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --read 4 rbl-after.bin
.\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --rawdeviceread 0 1024 bct-after.bin[/CODE]
The first 860160 bytes of [I]ebt-after.bin[/I] must equal [I]ebt\ebt-patched-v2-D62510c.bin[/I], and [I]rbl-after.bin[/I] must be identical to [I]rbl-before.bin[/I]. [I]bct-after.bin[/I] differs from [I]bct-before.bin[/I] by design: nvflash wrote the new bootloader hash. Keep it with your backups.

[SIZE="4"][B]Restore the stock bootloader[/B][/SIZE]

Enter APX, repeat Step 2, then use the same managed flow with the stock bootloader from the kit:
[CODE].\tools\nvflash.exe --resume --download 5 ebt\ebt-stock-D62510c.bin --sync[/CODE]

Do not restore only the original BCT while the patched EBT is installed. A full APX recovery procedure is documented at [URL="https://github.com/chijure/lg-d625-tegra4i-recovery"]lg-d625-tegra4i-recovery[/URL].

[SIZE="4"][B]Next step: TWRP[/B][/SIZE]

Optional: install a custom recovery, for example TWRP 3.1.1-1 for the D625 (posted in its own thread). With the patched bootloader you can test any recovery image first without installing it:
[CODE]fastboot boot recovery.img[/CODE]

[SIZE="4"][B]FAQ[/B][/SIZE]

[B]Does this wipe my data?[/B]
No. Nothing is erased, and fastboot oem unlock is not used.

[B]Does it work on the D620, D618 or other G2 Mini models?[/B]
No. Those use a Snapdragon 400 and a completely different bootloader. This is for the Tegra 4i [B]D625[/B] only.

[B]My firmware is not D62510c.[/B]
The script will refuse your bootloader. Do not try to force it.

[B]Is a one-click tool available?[/B]
No. The kit has the patched bootloader ready, but the backup and the hash check in Steps 3 and 4 are deliberately manual: they are what keeps a wrong firmware from being flashed.

[SIZE="4"][B]Technical details[/B][/SIZE]

Offsets, the BCT integrity analysis and the hardware evidence are in the repository: [URL="https://github.com/chijure-android-system-development/lg-d625-tegra-bootloader-patch/blob/main/BOOTLOADER_PATCH_ANALYSIS.md"]BOOTLOADER_PATCH_ANALYSIS.md[/URL].

